SANS @RISK: Threat Intelligence Briefing
Volume 24, Issue 39

THREAT IDENTIFIER:
Vulnerability: CVE-2024-6387
Vendor: OpenBSD / OpenSSH Project
Product: OpenSSH Server (sshd)
Affected Versions: 8.5p1 up to 9.8p1 (glibc-based Linux systems)
Vulnerability Type: Remote Code Execution / Signal Handler Race Condition
Attack Vector: NETWORK
MITRE ATT&CK Technique: T1190 (Exploit Public-Facing Application)
Exploitation Status: ACTIVE_IN_THE_WILD
Attack Outcome: EXPLOITED_SUCCESSFULLY
Affected Sector: Enterprise Cloud Providers, Telecommunications, Healthcare
Publication Date: 2026-09-17

ANALYSIS:
A critical signal handler race condition regression in OpenSSH server (sshd) allows unauthenticated remote attackers on the network to achieve arbitrary code execution with root privileges on 32-bit and 64-bit Linux server distributions. Threat intelligence sources confirm active automated scanning and exploitation frameworks targeting exposed SSH port 22.

RECOMMENDED ACTION:
- Upgrade OpenSSH to version 9.8p1 or newer immediately.
- If immediate patching is not possible, set 'LoginGraceTime 0' in sshd_config as temporary mitigation.
- NIST-PR.AC-1: Block internet-facing SSH access via perimeter firewall rules.

BUSINESS IMPACT:
High risk of infrastructure takeover. Expected enterprise breach exposure: ₹3,800,000.

Confidence: 0.94
